WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.165 --> 00:00:02.748
(gentle music)

00:00:06.429 --> 00:00:09.262
(grandiose music)

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:18.050
- [Narrator] Spinner dolphins.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:23.300
In the Pacific off the coast of Costa Rica

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.980
in Central America,
they're starting to gather

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.283
in great numbers.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:34.260
Spinners live in pods,
groups of individuals

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.343
linked by complex social relationships.

00:00:39.524 --> 00:00:42.607
(dolphins squeaking)

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.220
The dolphins constantly touch
and talk with one another

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.151
using a language of clicks and whistles.

00:00:54.151 --> 00:00:57.234
(dolphins squeaking)

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:03.793
They're shadowed by yellowfin tuna.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:10.083
The tuna rely on the
dolphins to find food.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:14.973
Everyone is searching for the same prey.

00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:22.220
Lanternfish, the most
numerous fish on the planet.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.970
(grandiose orchestral music)

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:31.290
No longer than your
finger, lanternfish spend

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.563
most of their time hiding in the deep.

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:37.630
So despite their astounding abundance,

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.133
we don't fish for them.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:45.713
They are one of the few
fish that still thrives.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:52.310
As they close in on their
prey, the dolphin pod

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:54.800
gets bigger and bigger.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.550
(grandiose orchestral music)

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.881
This pod could be 10,000 strong.

00:02:02.881 --> 00:02:06.631
(grandiose orchestral music)

00:02:13.481 --> 00:02:17.896
For every one at the
surface, 20 swim below.

00:02:17.896 --> 00:02:21.646
(grandiose orchestral music)

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:45.990
Using echolocation, a type of sonar,

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:49.100
the dolphins detect
lanternfish in the depths

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:50.811
and drive them to the surface.

00:02:50.811 --> 00:02:53.894
(dolphins squeaking)

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:08.920
They patrol the underside of the show

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.748
to stop their prey
escaping back to the deep.

00:03:11.748 --> 00:03:16.748
(dolphins squeaking)
(gentle orchestral music)

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:30.260
Once the dolphins have had their fill,

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:33.740
there's still plenty
more for other predators.

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:36.011
Because we don't fish for them,

00:03:36.011 --> 00:03:39.558
lanternfish and their predators thrive.

00:03:39.558 --> 00:03:42.391
(grandiose music)

00:03:45.654 --> 00:03:48.737
(dolphins squeaking)

